\chapter{C11 headers, implementation macros, and static inline}
\label{ch:92}
\index{C11 headers, implementation macros, and static inline}
\index{Kernels!implementation macros}
\index{Kernels!static inline}

Most MuLIB kernels expose interfaces in headers. ABI modules use a \OnxCodeTerm{MULIB\_IMPL\_...} switch in one C translation unit. Other modules are static-inline or static-data and need no implementation switch. Including a header in two downstream files is normal. Turning on the same external implementation in two downstream files can define the same symbol twice. An internal-linkage inline function needs explicit forwarding if a particular consumer expects an external symbol.

\section{Build a two-file C client}
Choose a documented ABI kernel and use its manual's exact switch and dependencies. Place the switch and include in one file. In the second, include the header without the switch and call a documented operation. Compile both as strict C11 and link them. Remove the implementation file to observe the missing definition, and compare with a static-inline kernel documented as usable directly from its header. Keep platform macros and integration profiles local to the documented implementation rather than exporting them as global compiler flags.

\section{Read a link failure as a placement error}
For a concrete experiment, make the entire contents of the provider file
the following two lines. The switch has to be visible when the header
is processed; putting it in a different source file cannot affect this
translation unit.
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language=C]
#define MULIB_IMPL_VEC
#include "mulib-vec.h"
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
Use the vector manual's example as the second file, removing only its
implementation switch. The client knows how large the vector record is
and can allocate one on the stack. Its calls to initialize, push, look
up and free the vector still require definitions at link time. With
the files named as below, try the two compile steps before the link:
\begin{OnxCodeBlock}[numbers=none,language={}]
cc -std=c11 -Iinclude -c provider.c -o provider.o
cc -std=c11 -Iinclude -c client.c -o client.o
cc client.o provider.o -o vec-client
\end{OnxCodeBlock}
Running the resulting client should reach the assertion on the stored
integer and finish successfully. Now link just the client object. A
missing definition here does not mean the compiler missed the include
directory: a missing header would have failed the earlier compile step.
Conversely, leaving the switch in the example while also linking the
provider makes both objects supply the vector functions. Fix their
placement rather than suppressing the linker's multiple-definition error.

Contrast the QVM core example: its translation unit includes the header
and directly calls inline helpers. No vector-like provider object is
missing from that build. Copying an internal helper's prototype into a
different client cannot make a global definition appear in an archive.

